++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"card-reply-s-length-is-task-specific-not","text":"card_reply's length is task-specific, not universal. The read-peers phase asks for 'one or two plain sentences per peer'; no other phase specifies a range.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"no-rule-in-the-codebase-says-card-reply-","text":"No rule in the codebase says card_reply 'must always be two or three plain sentences' as a universal constraint.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"a-task-mastermind-may-not-spawn-a-second","text":"A task mastermind may NOT spawn a second task mastermind yet, but this is conditional: 'depth stops at two UNTIL one measured run proves a three-deep tree can report failure upward' (sub-mastermind SKILL.md line 89).","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-no-nesting-yet-rule-is-experimental-","text":"The no-nesting-yet rule is experimental, not foundational—it exists because the claim has never been tested, not because nesting always breaks.","certainty":"likely"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"server-merge-mjs-is-the-one-serialized-w","text":"Server/merge.mjs is 'the one serialized way to land a worktree' (its header line 1–2), preventing two merges from running at once via a lock file.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"server-merge-mjs-itself-runs-git-merge-n","text":"Server/merge.mjs itself runs 'git merge --no-ff' for clean merges with no overlapping uncommitted work in the main tree (line 350).","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-review-gate-in-merge-mjs-line-85-is-","text":"The review gate in merge.mjs (line 85+) is a GATE, not a mandate to use merge.mjs for all merges—it only refuses branches sized 'light' or 'full' that lack a review.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"a-merge-of-size-none-only-css-or-docs-20","text":"A merge of size 'none' (only CSS or docs, 20 lines or fewer, no new file—per review.md line 60–61) bypasses the review gate entirely and presumably could use plain git merge.","certainty":"likely"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"card-reply-s-length-bends-in-every-phase","text":"Card_reply's length bends in every phase after read-peers: brief/revise/vote/names/implement phases give no sentence limit, only content direction.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-no-second-mastermind-rule-bends-when","text":"The no-second-mastermind rule bends when a measured run proves a three-level tree can report failure upward—this is explicitly the intended exit ramp.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-merge-via-merge-mjs-rule-bends-for-s","text":"The merge-via-merge.mjs rule bends for size 'none' (review gate skipped), and merge.mjs itself delegates to 'git merge --no-ff' when there's no conflict.","certainty":"likely"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"card-reply-text-for-direct-questions-sho","text":"card_reply text for direct questions should be one or two sentences maximum, rarely three — a confirmation, never substantive explanation. Substantive replies go on board cards as Note: prefixed entries, not in chat replies.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"a-task-mastermind-may-never-spawn-a-seco","text":"A task mastermind may never spawn a second task mastermind as of now. Depth stops at two (mastermind → minions, never mastermind → mastermind → minions) until one measured run proves a three-deep tree can report failure upward.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-two-level-depth-rule-is-explicitly-c","text":"The two-level depth rule is explicitly conditional: 'until one measured run proves a three-deep tree can report failure upward'. It is the current guard, not a permanent architectural law.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"a-minion-or-sub-mastermind-must-never-co","text":"A minion or sub-mastermind must never commit to the main branch or push. They work in a worktree, commit on their own branch, and hand the owner a branch name and diffstat. The merge step — running Server/merge.mjs — is the owner's responsibility, not the agent's.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"server-merge-mjs-is-the-mechanism-that-l","text":"Server/merge.mjs is the MECHANISM that lands branches — it reads task.jsonl, checks the review gate, and merges. Plain git merge is not forbidden; git merge is just not the agent's step to run. The process IS enforced: a minion that tries git merge on main breaks the shared tree.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"in-a-worktree-branch-a-sub-mastermind-is","text":"In a worktree branch, a sub-mastermind IS allowed to commit and may be required to. The 'never commit' rule applies only to the main branch. A worktree is isolated, so commits there do not touch shared state.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"when-a-substantive-explanation-is-needed","text":"When a substantive explanation is needed (not just a yes/no or confirmation), put it on a board card as a Note: entry, not in the chat reply. The reply itself stays to one line pointing at the card.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"a-sub-mastermind-spawning-minions-for-a-","text":"A sub-mastermind spawning minions for a task is normal and required. The 'never spawn a second mastermind' rule blocks nested masterminds (a mastermind spawning another mastermind for a sub-task), not minions spawning minions.","certainty":"likely"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-sub-mastermind-skill-md-says-a-lande","text":"The sub-mastermind SKILL.md says a Landed report is 'one screen' of plain sentences with links, and says numbers stay in the log; it never states a two-or-three-sentence rule for card_reply specifically, so that exact wording is likely a convention enforced elsewhere (e.g. the mastermind/every-prompt tier docs), not something found as a hard rule in these two files.","certainty":"open"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-skill-md-s-never-list-states-plainly","text":"The SKILL.md's Never list states plainly: 'spawn another task mastermind (depth stops at two until one measured run proves a three-deep tree can report failure upward)' — this is a settled, explicit hard rule with a named condition for when it could change (a measured run proving upward failure-reporting works at three deep).","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"the-stated-reason-for-the-depth-two-cap-","text":"The stated reason for the depth-two cap is about reporting failure upward through a background/nested-agent notification path, not about task scope or quality — so the edge case that would bend it is specifically a proven mechanism for a three-deep tree to surface a failure to the top, not just a 'this task is complex enough to need it' argument.","certainty":"likely"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"server-doc-review-md-describes-server-me","text":"Server/doc/review.md describes Server/merge.mjs as a hard gate: it 'refuses' a light or full branch with no review newer than its last commit, or with an unanswered [fix] finding — this reads as an enforced mechanical rule (a script refusing), not a convention, for branches that are light or full size.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"review-md-s-size-table-lists-none-only-c","text":"review.md's size table lists 'none' (only CSS or docs changed, 20 lines or fewer, no new file) as reviewed by nobody — implying the merge.mjs gate's review requirement is naturally moot for none-size changes, since there is no review to be newer than the last commit; this is the one clearly legitimate edge where 'every merge needs a review' does not bite, though the doc doesn't say merge.mjs explicitly special-cases it.","certainty":"likely"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"review-md-never-mentions-a-plain-git-mer","text":"review.md never mentions a plain 'git merge' as an alternative path at all, and describes merge.mjs purely in terms of what it refuses and reads (task.jsonl) — there is no stated escape hatch or override for skipping merge.mjs in favor of a plain git merge, so nothing in these two files shows where that rule bends; it may simply be absolute within this doc's scope.","certainty":"open"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"review-md-states-the-task-mastermind-may","text":"review.md states the task mastermind may lower the review size only with a logged '--why self-evident: <one line>' reason, while raising the size needs no reason — this is a settled asymmetric rule, and it's the clearest example in the doc of a place a strict-sounding classification (size) is explicitly allowed to bend, with the exact condition for bending spelled out.","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"review-md-is-explicit-that-the-four-turn","text":"review.md is explicit that the four-turn review process has 'one round of holds only — turn 4 is the last word, there is no turn 5,' which is a settled hard rule about the review flow (though separate from the merge-mechanism question).","certainty":"settled"}}
++{"fact":{"at":"2026-09-28T15:35:19-05:00","id":"review-md-flags-its-own-turns-model-as-b","text":"review.md flags its own turns model as built for exactly one finding pass per task, and calls a genuine second full review pass after settlement 'a known limit, not a defended-against case' — this is an admitted edge case where the review/merge machinery does not have a clean rule yet, rather than a place the rule is known to bend safely.","certainty":"open"}}
